#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:1
msgid "Invoking `justfile`s in Other Directories"
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:3
msgid ""
"If the first argument passed to `just` contains a `/`, then the following "
"occurs:"
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:6
msgid "The argument is split at the last `/`."
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:8
msgid ""
"The part before the last `/` is treated as a directory. `just` will start "
"its search for the `justfile` there, instead of in the current directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:11
msgid ""
"The part after the last slash is treated as a normal argument, or ignored if "
"it is empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:14
msgid ""
"This may seem a little strange, but it’s useful if you wish to run a command "
"in a `justfile` that is in a subdirectory."
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:17
msgid ""
"For example, if you are in a directory which contains a subdirectory named "
"`foo`, which contains a `justfile` with the recipe `build`, which is also "
"the default recipe, the following are all equivalent:"
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:27
msgid ""
"Additional recipes after the first are sought in the same `justfile`. For "
"example, the following are both equivalent:"
msgstr ""

#: src/invoking-justfiles-in-other-directories.md:35
msgid "And will both invoke recipes `a` and `b` in `foo/justfile`."
msgstr ""
